// SV-OS Content Richness Pass — File 4 of 5: OS + DB + Networks (19 nodes total). Status: COMPLETE — 19/19 (OS 7, DB 6, Networks 6). Format: JSONL — one compact JSON object per line, UTF-8. Schema: {title, summary, learning_outcomes[3], common_mistakes[3-4], worked_example:{setup, steps[], result}}. Strip this comment line before parsing.
{"title":"Processes & Process Management","summary":"A process is a program in execution — distinct from the program itself (a static file on disk), a process is a dynamic entity with its own address space, execution state, and resources (open files, allocated memory). Every process moves through a defined set of states: new (being created), ready (waiting for CPU time), running (actively executing), waiting/blocked (waiting for an event like I/O completion), and terminated (finished) — the scheduler moves processes between ready and running, while I/O or event completion moves a process from waiting back to ready. The OS tracks each process's state using a Process Control Block (PCB), holding the process ID, current state, program counter, register values, memory management information, and scheduling information — when the OS switches from one process to another (a context switch), it saves the current process's CPU state into its PCB and loads the next process's saved state, an operation that itself consumes CPU time and contributes zero useful work. A process's address space is typically organized into segments: code (instructions), data (global/static variables), heap (dynamically allocated memory, growing upward), and stack (function call frames and locals, growing downward) — keeping these separate lets the OS enforce memory protection and detect errors like stack overflow (the stack growing into the heap). Creating a new process (via a call like fork in Unix-like systems) typically duplicates the parent's address space into a new child process, which then usually replaces its memory image with a different program via an exec-style call, separating 'create a process' from 'run a different program in it' as two distinct steps.","learning_outcomes":["Given a sequence of process events (I/O request, I/O completion, scheduler dispatch), trace which process-state transitions occur.","Explain what information a PCB holds and why each piece is necessary to correctly resume a process after a context switch.","Describe the fork-then-exec pattern for process creation and explain why they are separated into two distinct system calls."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming a running process goes directly back to running after using its time slice — a preempted process moves to ready, not directly back to running.","Confusing a program with a process — a program is a static file; a process is the running instance, and the same program can run as multiple independent processes simultaneously, each with its own PCB.","Assuming context switching is free or instantaneous — saving and restoring full CPU state takes real time and contributes no useful computation, which is why excessive context switching degrades throughput.","Assuming fork() runs a different program directly — fork() only duplicates the calling process; running a different program requires a subsequent exec-style call in the child, a separate step."],"worked_example":{"setup":"A process starts in 'new'. Trace its state after: (1) admitted by the OS, (2) dispatched to CPU, (3) issues an I/O request, (4) I/O completes, (5) dispatched again, (6) finishes execution.","steps":["Event 1 (admitted): 'new' → 'ready'.","Event 2 (dispatched): 'ready' → 'running'.","Event 3 (I/O request): 'running' → 'waiting'.","Event 4 (I/O completes): 'waiting' → 'ready'.","Event 5 (dispatched again): 'ready' → 'running'.","Event 6 (finishes): 'running' → 'terminated'."],"result":"Full state sequence: new → ready → running → waiting → ready → running → terminated — six transitions driven by three distinct causes (OS admission, scheduler dispatch, I/O request/completion), showing only specific events move a process between specific state pairs."}}
{"title":"Threads & Concurrency","summary":"A thread is an independent sequence of execution within a process, sharing the process's address space (code, data, heap) with sibling threads, but each thread has its own stack, program counter, and register set — this shared memory makes inter-thread communication cheap (read/write shared variables directly) compared to inter-process communication, but also makes threads more dangerous to get wrong, since uncoordinated access to shared data can corrupt it. Because threads share an address space, creating and switching between threads of the same process is typically much cheaper than doing so for processes, since there's no need to set up separate memory mappings — the core reason multithreaded programs suit tasks that benefit from parallel work within one logical program, like a web server handling multiple client connections concurrently. Concurrency and parallelism are related but distinct: concurrency means multiple tasks can make progress over the same time period (potentially by interleaving on a single core), while parallelism means multiple tasks execute at the exact same instant on multiple cores — a single-core system can be concurrent but never truly parallel, while a multi-core system can be both. A race condition occurs when a program's correctness depends on the relative timing of multiple threads' operations on shared data — for example, if two threads both read a shared counter's current value, each independently increment their local copy, and write it back, one thread's increment can be silently lost, because the read-modify-write sequence wasn't treated as a single indivisible operation. Coordinating threads to avoid race conditions is the central challenge of concurrent programming, requiring the explicit synchronization mechanisms covered as their own topic.","learning_outcomes":["Explain what a thread shares with sibling threads (address space) versus what it keeps private (stack, registers, program counter), and why this affects communication cost and safety.","Distinguish concurrency from parallelism with a concrete example of each, including whether a single-core or multi-core scenario is concurrent, parallel, both, or neither.","Identify a race condition in a given short multithreaded code snippet involving a shared variable, and explain the specific interleaving that produces an incorrect result."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming concurrency requires multiple CPU cores — a single core can be concurrent by interleaving between threads, even though it can never achieve true parallelism with just one core.","Assuming threads are always safer or more dangerous than processes in an absolute sense — threads' shared memory makes communication cheap but requires coordination, while processes' isolation prevents this specific bug class but makes communication more expensive.","Assuming a simple operation like 'increment a counter' is atomic by default — 'counter++' typically involves a separate read, modify, and write, and without synchronization two threads can interleave between these steps, silently losing an update.","Believing a race condition will always be caught during testing — race conditions depend on timing that can vary between runs, so a program can pass many test runs and still contain one that surfaces unpredictably in production."],"worked_example":{"setup":"A shared counter starts at 10. Threads T1 and T2 each execute 'counter = counter + 1' without synchronization, interleaving as: T1 reads (10); T2 reads (10); T1 writes 11; T2 writes 11.","steps":["T1 reads the shared counter: T1's local copy = 10.","T2 reads the shared counter before T1 writes back: T2's local copy = 10.","T1 computes 10+1=11 and writes counter=11.","T2 computes its stale local copy 10+1=11 (unaware of T1's update) and writes counter=11, overwriting T1's result with the same value."],"result":"Final counter value is 11, even though two increments occurred — the correct result after two increments from 10 should be 12; T2's update effectively overwrote T1's, silently losing one increment purely due to interleaving."}}
{"title":"CPU Scheduling","summary":"CPU scheduling decides which ready process runs next, and different algorithms optimize for different, sometimes competing goals: throughput (processes completed per unit time), turnaround time (arrival to completion), waiting time (time in the ready queue, not running), and response time (arrival to first getting the CPU). First-Come-First-Served (FCFS) runs processes in arrival order to completion, simple but prone to the 'convoy effect' where one long process delays every process behind it; Shortest Job First (SJF) always picks the shortest remaining burst time, provably optimal for minimizing average waiting time among non-preemptive algorithms, but requires knowing burst times in advance and can starve long processes. Round Robin gives each process a fixed time quantum before preempting and moving to the next process in a circular queue, guaranteeing fairness and good response time regardless of burst length, but its average waiting time depends heavily on quantum size — too large and it degenerates toward FCFS, too small and context-switch overhead dominates. Priority scheduling runs the highest-priority ready process first, but can starve low-priority processes indefinitely, commonly solved with aging (gradually increasing a waiting process's priority the longer it waits). Because no single algorithm optimizes every goal simultaneously, real operating systems typically use a combination (like a multilevel feedback queue) approximating the benefits of several strategies.","learning_outcomes":["Given a set of processes with arrival and burst times, compute the Gantt chart, waiting time, and turnaround time for FCFS, SJF, and Round Robin scheduling.","Explain the convoy effect in FCFS and the starvation risk in SJF and priority scheduling, and identify how Round Robin and aging address related fairness problems.","Given a Round Robin schedule with a specified quantum, trace execution and preemption, and explain how quantum size affects response time versus context-switch overhead."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming SJF is always achievable in practice — it requires knowing or accurately estimating burst time in advance, which is often not truly known until a process finishes.","Forgetting Round Robin still preempts a process at the quantum boundary even if it needs more than one quantum, regardless of how close it was to finishing.","Computing waiting time as just the burst time rather than turnaround time minus burst time — waiting time specifically excludes time spent actually running, a common arithmetic error.","Assuming a smaller Round Robin quantum is always better with no downside — a smaller quantum improves response time but increases context-switch count, and since each switch has overhead, an excessively small quantum can reduce throughput."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Three processes arrive at t=0 with burst times P1=6, P2=3, P3=8. Compute the Gantt chart, waiting time, and average waiting time using non-preemptive SJF.","steps":["SJF orders by burst time ascending: P2(3), P1(6), P3(8).","Gantt chart: P2 runs 0→3; P1 runs 3→9; P3 runs 9→17.","Waiting time (start − arrival, arrival=0 for all): P2=0−0=0; P1=3−0=3; P3=9−0=9.","Average waiting time = (0+3+9)/3 = 4."],"result":"Running the shortest job first minimizes average waiting time to 4 — running in arrival order (P1,P2,P3) instead would give waiting times 0,6,9, averaging 5, confirming SJF's provable optimality for minimizing average waiting time when burst times are known in advance."}}
{"title":"Synchronization & Deadlocks","summary":"The critical section problem asks how to ensure only one thread at a time executes code accessing shared data (the critical section), satisfying mutual exclusion (no two threads in their critical section simultaneously), progress, and bounded waiting — a mutex (lock) is the simplest solution: a thread acquires the lock before entering and releases it after, with the acquire operation itself guaranteed atomic. A semaphore generalizes this with an integer counter and two atomic operations, wait/P (decrement, blocking if it would go negative) and signal/V (increment, waking a blocked thread if any) — a binary semaphore (0 or 1) behaves like a mutex, while a counting semaphore controls access to a pool of several identical resources, such as a fixed-size connection pool. A deadlock is a state where processes each wait for a resource held by another in the same set, with none able to proceed — this requires all four Coffman conditions simultaneously: mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait (holding one resource while waiting for another), no preemption, and circular wait — breaking any single one is sufficient to prevent deadlock. Deadlock handling falls into three strategies: prevention (structurally ensuring one condition can never hold, e.g. a fixed global resource-acquisition order eliminating circular wait), avoidance (allowing all four conditions but using runtime information, like the Banker's algorithm, to only grant a request if the resulting state is still 'safe'), and detection-and-recovery (periodically checking via cycle detection in a resource-allocation graph, then recovering by preempting resources or terminating a process). Because prevention and avoidance both cost throughput while detection-and-recovery risks real deadlocks occurring before they're caught, many practical systems simply ignore the problem (the 'ostrich algorithm'), relying on deadlocks being rare or resolved by a system restart.","learning_outcomes":["Given a critical section scenario, explain how a mutex enforces mutual exclusion, and identify a scenario where a counting semaphore (rather than binary) is appropriate.","Given a resource-allocation scenario, determine whether all four Coffman conditions hold, and identify which single condition a proposed prevention strategy breaks.","Distinguish deadlock prevention, avoidance, and detection-and-recovery by explaining what each guarantees and what it costs."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming a semaphore's wait/signal operations don't need to be atomic themselves — without atomicity the counter itself could become corrupted, defeating the purpose of using a semaphore.","Assuming deadlock can occur with only three of the four Coffman conditions holding — deadlock specifically requires all four simultaneously; if even one is absent, that cyclic-waiting state cannot occur.","Confusing deadlock prevention with avoidance — prevention makes one condition permanently impossible, while avoidance still allows all four to potentially arise but checks each request against a 'safe state' test before granting it.","Assuming a resource-allocation graph cycle always proves deadlock — true only when each resource type has a single instance; with multiple instances, a cycle is necessary but not always sufficient."],"worked_example":{"setup":"P1 holds Resource A and requests B; P2 holds B and requests A; neither resource is shareable, neither process releases until it gets what it's waiting for. Check all four Coffman conditions.","steps":["Mutual exclusion: A and B cannot be shared — holds.","Hold-and-wait: P1 holds A while waiting for B, P2 holds B while waiting for A — holds.","No preemption: neither resource can be forcibly taken — holds.","Circular wait: P1→B(held by P2)→P2→A(held by P1)→P1 forms a cycle — holds."],"result":"All four Coffman conditions hold simultaneously, so this is a genuine deadlock — neither process can ever proceed; breaking just one condition (e.g. requiring both resources be requested atomically together, eliminating hold-and-wait) would have prevented it."}}
{"title":"Virtual Memory","summary":"Virtual memory gives each process the illusion of a large, private, contiguous address space, independent of physical RAM size — achieved through paging: both virtual and physical memory are divided into fixed-size chunks (pages and frames, typically 4KB), and a per-process page table maps virtual page numbers to physical frame numbers, so every memory access is translated before touching RAM. Because a full page-table lookup on every access would be too slow, a Translation Lookaside Buffer (TLB) — a small, fast hardware cache of recent translations — is checked first; a TLB hit avoids the full page-table walk, while a TLB miss requires walking the table (and caching the result), which is why TLB hit rate has an outsized effect on performance. A page fault occurs when a process accesses a virtual page not currently mapped to a physical frame (never loaded, or swapped out to disk) — the OS handles it by finding or evicting a frame, loading the page from disk, updating the page table, and resuming the faulting instruction. When memory is full and a page must be evicted, page replacement algorithms decide which: FIFO evicts the oldest-loaded page (simple, but can suffer Belady's anomaly, where adding more physical frames can paradoxically increase page faults for certain access patterns), while Least Recently Used (LRU) evicts the page unaccessed the longest, generally performing much better by exploiting temporal locality, though the theoretical (unimplementable) benchmark is Belady's Optimal algorithm, evicting whichever page won't be needed for the longest time in the future. Thrashing occurs when a system spends so much time handling page faults that it makes little progress on actual computation, typically because too many processes compete for too little physical memory relative to their combined working sets — the standard fix is reducing the degree of multiprogramming, counterintuitively fewer concurrent processes, not more.","learning_outcomes":["Explain how a virtual address is translated to a physical address using a page table, and describe the TLB's performance role in this process.","Given a page access sequence and a fixed number of physical frames, simulate FIFO and LRU page replacement and count the resulting page faults.","Explain Belady's anomaly and why it specifically affects FIFO but not LRU or Optimal, and describe what causes thrashing and its standard remedy."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming more physical frames always reduce page faults — true for LRU and Optimal, but FIFO can exhibit Belady's anomaly where adding frames paradoxically increases faults.","Assuming a TLB miss means a page fault occurred — a TLB miss only means the translation wasn't cached and requires a page table walk; the page may still be present in physical memory, a much cheaper event than a real page fault.","Believing Belady's Optimal is a real, usable policy — it requires knowing the future access sequence in advance, impossible in a real system; it exists purely as a theoretical benchmark.","Responding to thrashing by running more concurrent processes to 'keep the CPU busy' — this worsens thrashing by further dividing limited memory; the correct fix is reducing the degree of multiprogramming."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Using the reference string 1,2,3,4,1,2,5,1,2,3,4,5, compare FIFO page replacement with 3 physical frames versus 4, to demonstrate Belady's anomaly.","steps":["With 3 frames: references 1,2,3,4,1,2,5 each fault in turn as the oldest resident page is repeatedly evicted (7 faults); the next two references, 1 and 2, are hits (still resident); then 3 and 4 each fault again (2 more); the final reference, 5, is a hit. Total: 9 faults.","With 4 frames: references 1,2,3,4 all load without eviction (4 faults, frames now full); the next two, 1 and 2, are hits; then 5 faults (evicting 1), and the remaining references 1,2,3,4,5 each fault in turn as each needed page has just been evicted by the one before it. Total: 10 faults."],"result":"Adding a physical frame (3→4) increased the fault count (9→10) — a direct demonstration of Belady's anomaly; LRU and Optimal are proven immune to this ('stack algorithms' whose fault count can never increase with more frames), which is exactly why FIFO's vulnerability is considered a practical weakness."}}
{"title":"File Systems","summary":"A file system provides the abstraction of named, persistent files and directories on top of a raw storage device's linear sequence of fixed-size blocks, handling how a file's data is laid out across those blocks and found again later. Contiguous allocation stores a file's blocks sequentially, giving excellent read performance but suffering external fragmentation and requiring a file's maximum size to be known in advance; linked allocation stores each block with a pointer to the next block of the same file, eliminating fragmentation and allowing free growth, but making random access slow (finding block k requires traversing k pointers) and risking data loss if a pointer is corrupted; indexed allocation (used by most real file systems) gives each file an index block holding pointers to all its data blocks, combining fast random access with flexible growth, at the cost of the index block's own space. A Unix-style inode is the per-file metadata structure holding owner, permissions, timestamps, size, and pointers to data blocks (often direct pointers for small files and one or more levels of indirect pointers for larger ones, so maximum file size isn't hard-capped by a fixed-size inode) — critically, filenames aren't stored in the inode but in directory entries mapping a name to an inode number, which is what allows the same file's data to have multiple names (hard links) pointing at the same inode. Free space is tracked with a structure like a bitmap (one bit per block) or a free list, and the directory structure itself is typically just a specially-formatted file whose 'data' is a list of (filename, inode number) pairs. Because a crash during a multi-step operation (like allocating a block and updating an inode) can leave the file system inconsistent if interrupted partway through, many modern file systems use journaling: writing intended changes to a separate log before making them, so after a crash the file system can replay or roll back incomplete operations from the journal, rather than requiring a slow, full-disk consistency scan.","learning_outcomes":["Compare contiguous, linked, and indexed file allocation methods in terms of random-access speed, fragmentation, and flexibility of file growth.","Explain what information a Unix-style inode holds (and doesn't hold), and explain how hard links are possible given that filenames live in directory entries.","Explain why journaling improves crash consistency compared to no journaling, and describe what the file system does with the journal after an unclean shutdown."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming a file's name is stored in the inode itself — the name lives in a directory entry mapping to an inode number, which is exactly what makes hard links possible.","Assuming linked and indexed allocation have similar random-access performance — linked requires traversing pointers from the start (O(k)), while indexed looks up any block's pointer directly (O(1)).","Believing contiguous allocation has no real downsides given its fast sequential access — it suffers external fragmentation over time and requires knowing a file's eventual size in advance.","Assuming journaling eliminates any possibility of data loss after a crash — it protects the file system's own structural consistency, not necessarily unsaved application data never flushed to disk before the crash."],"worked_example":{"setup":"A simplified inode has 4 direct block pointers and one single-indirect pointer; each block is 1KB, and the indirect block holds 8 pointers to other 1KB blocks. Compute the maximum addressable file size.","steps":["Direct pointers: 4 × 1KB = 4KB addressable directly.","Single indirect pointer: points to an indirect block, which itself holds 8 pointers to 8 separate 1KB data blocks.","Capacity added by the indirect pointer: 8 × 1KB = 8KB.","Total maximum file size: 4KB (direct) + 8KB (indirect) = 12KB."],"result":"This inode structure addresses a maximum file size of 12KB — a file larger than 4KB requires the indirect pointer, illustrating why inodes use direct pointers for small files (fast, no indirection) and indirect pointers to extend addressable size for larger ones."}}
{"title":"I/O & Device Management","summary":"I/O and device management is the OS layer letting the kernel communicate with the enormous variety of physical hardware through a uniform set of abstractions, so higher-level code doesn't need to know each device's specifics. Devices are broadly block devices (transfer fixed-size chunks, support random access, like disks) or character devices (transfer a stream of individual bytes with no addressable structure, like a keyboard), and a device driver is the device-specific module translating generic OS I/O requests into the exact commands a particular device understands. There are three main strategies for the CPU to perform I/O: polling repeatedly checks (busy-waits on) a device's status register, wasting CPU cycles but requiring no special hardware; interrupt-driven I/O lets the CPU do other work and responds only when the device signals completion via a hardware interrupt, far more efficient than polling but still requiring the CPU to handle the actual data transfer word by word; Direct Memory Access (DMA) lets a separate controller handle the entire transfer between device and memory directly, interrupting the CPU only once the whole transfer completes, freeing the CPU almost entirely and making it standard for high-throughput devices like disks and network cards. I/O operations are typically buffered — data staged in a memory buffer rather than transferred directly to its final destination one unit at a time — smoothing out speed mismatches between a fast CPU and a slow device, and letting the CPU continue other work while a slower operation completes in the background. Because I/O devices are typically orders of magnitude slower than the CPU, well-designed I/O subsystems focus on minimizing the CPU's direct involvement in actual data movement (the progression from polling to interrupts to DMA), reserving CPU attention for the comparatively rare moments a decision is actually needed.","learning_outcomes":["Classify a given I/O device as block or character, and explain what distinguishes the two categories.","Compare polling, interrupt-driven I/O, and DMA in terms of CPU involvement, and identify which is most appropriate for a high-throughput device like a disk.","Explain the role of a device driver in letting the OS present a uniform interface to hardware."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming interrupt-driven I/O eliminates all CPU involvement in a transfer — the CPU is freed from busy-waiting but still handles the actual data movement itself unless DMA is used.","Assuming polling is simply 'worse' with no legitimate use case — it requires no special interrupt hardware and can have lower latency for devices that are ready quickly and predictably.","Believing DMA removes the CPU from I/O entirely — the CPU still initiates the transfer (setting up source, destination, length) and handles the single completion interrupt.","Assuming all devices suit the same I/O strategy — large, predictable transfers (disks) benefit from DMA, while small, irregular input (a keyboard) is often handled adequately with interrupt-driven I/O alone."],"worked_example":{"setup":"A program requests reading 4KB from disk into memory. Trace the steps using DMA-based I/O, noting whether the CPU or the DMA controller is responsible at each step.","steps":["CPU: sets up the transfer by programming the DMA controller with source (disk address), destination (memory address), and length (4KB) — a small, fixed setup cost.","CPU: is now free to execute other processes entirely unrelated to this I/O operation.","DMA controller: independently manages the entire 4KB transfer without CPU involvement in moving the bytes.","DMA controller: once the transfer completes, signals a single interrupt to the CPU."],"result":"The CPU is involved only at the start (brief setup) and end (one interrupt), while the DMA controller handles the entire 4KB data movement — contrasted with interrupt-driven I/O without DMA, where the CPU would need to respond repeatedly to move each unit itself, making DMA the clear choice for large, high-throughput transfers."}}
{"title":"Relational Model & SQL","summary":"The relational model represents data as relations (tables), each a set of tuples (rows) sharing the same attributes (columns), with each attribute drawn from a defined domain (type) — a relation's schema defines its structure, while its instance is the actual current set of rows. A primary key uniquely identifies each tuple in a relation, while a foreign key is an attribute in one relation referencing another's primary key, enforcing referential integrity by ensuring a foreign key value always corresponds to an actual existing row (or is null, if permitted). SQL is the standard language for relational databases, with SELECT-FROM-WHERE as its core pattern: SELECT specifies columns, FROM specifies tables, WHERE filters rows — and JOIN operations combine rows from multiple tables, with INNER JOIN returning only matched rows, LEFT JOIN returning all rows from the left table plus matches from the right (NULLs where unmatched), and similarly for RIGHT and FULL OUTER JOIN. Underlying SQL is relational algebra, a formal set of operations (selection σ filters rows, projection π selects columns, join ⋈ combines relations) giving the relational model a precise mathematical foundation that a database's query optimizer actually reasons about when deciding how to execute a query efficiently. Because the relational model separates the logical structure of data from how it's physically stored, applications can query declaratively (describing what result is wanted) rather than procedurally, leaving the database engine free to choose and change the actual execution strategy.","learning_outcomes":["Given a relational schema with primary and foreign keys, write a SQL query using SELECT-FROM-WHERE and an appropriate JOIN to answer a question spanning multiple tables.","Distinguish INNER, LEFT, RIGHT, and FULL OUTER JOIN by predicting which rows (including any NULL-padded rows) each produces for a small example.","Translate a simple SQL query into the equivalent relational algebra expression using selection (σ), projection (π), and join (⋈)."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming a foreign key constraint prevents any NULL in that column — a foreign key can typically still be NULL; what it prevents is a non-NULL value that doesn't match an existing row.","Confusing LEFT JOIN with INNER JOIN when a match doesn't exist — INNER JOIN drops unmatched rows entirely, while LEFT JOIN keeps every left-table row, filling unmatched columns with NULL.","Writing a WHERE clause that inadvertently filters out the NULL-padded rows a LEFT JOIN was meant to preserve — a condition like 'WHERE right.col = value' is unknown (not true) for NULLs, silently reverting to INNER JOIN behavior.","Treating primary key uniqueness as something to manually check in application code — the database itself enforces it as a constraint, rejecting violating inserts or updates."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Students(student_id,name): (1,'Alice'),(2,'Bob'),(3,'Carol'). Enrollments(student_id,course): (1,'Math'),(1,'Physics'),(2,'Math'). Write a query listing each student's name and course, including students with no enrollments.","steps":["Query: SELECT Students.name, Enrollments.course FROM Students LEFT JOIN Enrollments ON Students.student_id = Enrollments.student_id.","student_id=1 (Alice): two matches (Math, Physics) — two output rows.","student_id=2 (Bob): one match (Math) — one output row.","student_id=3 (Carol): no match; LEFT JOIN still includes Carol with course=NULL."],"result":"Query returns 4 rows: (Alice,Math), (Alice,Physics), (Bob,Math), (Carol,NULL) — Carol appears despite no enrollment specifically because LEFT JOIN preserves every left-table row, which INNER JOIN would have dropped, returning only 3 rows."}}
{"title":"Normalization & Schema Design","summary":"Normalization structures a relational schema to minimize data redundancy and avoid update, insertion, and deletion anomalies — problems arising when the same fact is stored in multiple places, or a fact can't be recorded without an unrelated fact also being present. The normal forms build progressively: First Normal Form (1NF) requires every attribute to hold a single atomic value; Second Normal Form (2NF) additionally requires every non-key attribute depend on the entire primary key, not just part of it (relevant only when the key is composite) — a partial dependency violates 2NF; Third Normal Form (3NF) further requires every non-key attribute depend only on the primary key, not on another non-key attribute (eliminating transitive dependencies). These rules are formalized using functional dependencies — notation X→Y meaning knowing X's value always determines Y's — with each normal form defined by which kinds of dependencies are and aren't allowed. Consider an unnormalized table storing (StudentID, StudentName, CourseID, CourseName) together: it suffers an update anomaly (changing a course's name requires updating every enrolled student's row), an insertion anomaly (a new course can't be recorded until a student enrolls), and a deletion anomaly (deleting the last student in a course deletes all record of the course) — splitting into separate Student, Course, and Enrollment tables eliminates all three by removing the underlying redundancy. Normalization isn't free: a fully normalized schema often needs more JOINs to reconstruct related data, which is why some systems deliberately denormalize for read-heavy workloads where query performance matters more than eliminating every possible anomaly.","learning_outcomes":["Given a table with repeating groups, convert it to 1NF, and given a table with a composite key, identify any partial dependencies violating 2NF.","Identify a transitive dependency in a given table's attributes and normalize it to 3NF by splitting it into separate relations.","Given an unnormalized table, identify a concrete update, insertion, and deletion anomaly, and explain how normalization eliminates each."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming 2NF violations can occur with any primary key — a partial dependency is only possible with a composite key; a single-attribute key automatically satisfies 2NF if it satisfies 1NF.","Confusing a transitive dependency (3NF's concern) with a partial dependency (2NF's concern) — the former involves a non-key attribute depending on another non-key attribute, the latter involves depending on only part of a composite key.","Assuming normalization has no costs and should always be maximized — a fully normalized schema often needs more JOINs, hurting read performance, which is why some systems deliberately denormalize.","Believing normalization eliminates the need for foreign keys — normalization avoids redundancy-driven anomalies, but referential integrity between the resulting tables still needs separate enforcement."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Enrollment(StudentID,StudentName,CourseID,CourseName) with composite key (StudentID,CourseID) has rows (1,'Alice','C1','Databases'),(1,'Alice','C2','Networks'),(2,'Bob','C1','Databases'). Identify the violation, normalize, and show the update anomaly is fixed.","steps":["Identify functional dependencies: StudentID→StudentName and CourseID→CourseName — each depends on only part of the composite key, a partial dependency violating 2NF.","Decompose: Student(StudentID,StudentName): (1,'Alice'),(2,'Bob'); Course(CourseID,CourseName): ('C1','Databases'),('C2','Networks'); Enrollment(StudentID,CourseID): (1,'C1'),(1,'C2'),(2,'C1').","Verify: StudentName now depends fully on StudentID (single-attribute key) in Student; CourseName fully on CourseID in Course — no partial or transitive dependency remains.","To rename C1 to 'Database Systems', update exactly one row: Course's row for C1."],"result":"In the original table, renaming C1 required updating two rows (Alice's and Bob's); in the normalized schema, it requires updating exactly one row, and every query joining Enrollment with Course automatically reflects the change — eliminating the update anomaly."}}
{"title":"Transactions & ACID","summary":"A transaction is a sequence of database operations treated as a single logical unit of work, which either completes entirely (commits) or has no effect at all (rolls back) — this guarantee, plus three others, is summarized by ACID. Atomicity guarantees a transaction's operations are indivisible: if any part fails, the entire transaction rolls back as if none of it happened. Consistency guarantees a transaction moves the database from one valid state to another, respecting all defined constraints; a transaction violating a constraint is rejected rather than committed. Isolation guarantees concurrently executing transactions don't interfere with each other's intermediate states, as if run one at a time — but full isolation (serializability) is expensive, so databases offer weaker levels: Read Uncommitted allows dirty reads (seeing another transaction's uncommitted, possibly-rolled-back changes); Read Committed prevents dirty reads but allows non-repeatable reads (re-reading a row returns a different value if another transaction committed a change in between); Repeatable Read additionally prevents non-repeatable reads but can still allow phantom reads (a re-run query returns additional rows a concurrent transaction inserted); Serializable prevents all three by ensuring transactions behave exactly as if executed one at a time. Durability guarantees that once a transaction commits, its changes survive any subsequent crash, typically implemented by writing changes to non-volatile storage (often via a write-ahead log) before acknowledging the commit.","learning_outcomes":["Explain each ACID property with a concrete example of what could go wrong in a transaction if that property were absent.","Given a scenario with two concurrent transactions, identify whether it exhibits a dirty read, non-repeatable read, or phantom read, and identify the weakest isolation level that would prevent it.","Explain why Serializable isolation, despite the strongest guarantee, isn't always used by default, referencing its performance cost relative to weaker levels."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming Repeatable Read prevents phantom reads — it only guarantees rows already read won't change on a re-read; it doesn't prevent a concurrent transaction's newly inserted rows from appearing in a re-executed range query.","Confusing a dirty read with a non-repeatable read — a dirty read involves seeing uncommitted data, a non-repeatable read involves seeing a different value on a second read of already-committed data because another transaction committed in between.","Assuming Atomicity and Consistency are the same guarantee — Atomicity is about the transaction succeeding or failing entirely, while Consistency is specifically about the resulting state respecting defined constraints.","Believing Durability is guaranteed the instant operations finish executing rather than at commit — changes are only guaranteed to survive a crash once the commit is acknowledged, typically after being written to durable storage."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Transaction T1 updates a balance from $100 to $150 but hasn't committed. T2, under Read Uncommitted isolation, reads the balance during this window. T1 then rolls back due to an error. Trace what T2 saw.","steps":["T1 begins: updates the balance to $150 but has not yet committed.","T2, under Read Uncommitted, reads the balance while T1's change is uncommitted: T2 sees $150.","T1 encounters an error and rolls back — the balance reverts to $100.","T2 has already used the value $150, a value that, as of the rollback, was never a committed balance."],"result":"T2 read a 'dirty' value ($150) later rolled back and never actually committed — possible only because Read Uncommitted permits reading uncommitted changes; Read Committed (or stronger) would have made T2 see $100 until T1's update actually commits."}}
{"title":"Indexing (B-Tree, Hash)","summary":"A database index is an auxiliary data structure that speeds up data retrieval by avoiding a full table scan for queries filtering or sorting on the indexed column — the trade-off is that every index must be maintained on every insert, update, or delete to the underlying table, so indexes speed up reads at some cost to write performance and storage. A B-tree (or more precisely, in most real databases, a B+ tree) index keeps indexed values in sorted order across a shallow, wide, self-balancing tree, giving O(log n) lookup, insertion, and deletion, and critically supporting efficient range queries by traversing the tree's sorted leaves, which is why B-tree indexes are the default choice for most columns in most relational databases. A hash index instead maps each value to a location via a hash function, giving average O(1) exact-match lookup — faster than a B-tree for equality — but cannot support range queries at all, since a hash function deliberately scatters similar values to unrelated locations, destroying ordering information. Databases distinguish a clustered index, where the table's actual row data is physically stored in the index's sorted order (so a table can have at most one), from a non-clustered (secondary) index, storing a separate structure mapping values to pointers/row locators, of which a table can have many. Because every additional index adds write overhead and storage cost, index design is a genuine trade-off — indexing a frequently-filtered column speeds up those queries, but indexing every column indiscriminately can slow writes significantly without proportional read benefit for rarely-queried columns.","learning_outcomes":["Explain why a B-tree (or B+ tree) index supports efficient range queries while a hash index does not, referencing how each structure organizes values.","Given a table and common query patterns (equality vs range vs sorting), recommend an appropriate index type and justify the choice.","Explain the trade-off between clustered and non-clustered indexes, including why a table can have only one clustered index but multiple non-clustered ones."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming a hash index is always faster than a B-tree — it's faster specifically for exact-match lookups, but a B-tree is required for range queries, sorting, or partial-match queries.","Believing adding more indexes only ever helps — every index adds overhead to every write, so indiscriminately indexing every column can significantly slow write-heavy workloads for limited read benefit.","Assuming a table can have multiple clustered indexes — rows are physically stored in only one order, so only one clustered index is possible per table.","Forgetting an index only helps a query that actually uses the indexed column in a supported way (e.g. in WHERE or JOIN) — creating an index doesn't automatically speed up every query touching that table."],"worked_example":{"setup":"A table has 1 million rows with an indexed 'age' column. Compare a B-tree versus a hash index for (a) 'WHERE age = 25' and (b) 'WHERE age BETWEEN 20 AND 30'.","steps":["Query (a) with a hash index: the hash function computes 25's location directly, average O(1) time regardless of table size.","Query (a) with a B-tree: the tree is traversed root to leaf, roughly log₂(1,000,000)≈20 comparisons.","Query (b) with a B-tree: traverse once to locate age=20, then read sorted leaf nodes sequentially forward until age exceeds 30.","Query (b) with a hash index: values 20-30 are scattered to unrelated locations by the hash function, so the index cannot service this range query at all, forcing a full table scan."],"result":"For the equality query, the hash index's O(1) average lookup beats the B-tree's O(log n); for the range query, the B-tree answers directly via sorted traversal while the hash index cannot service it at all — illustrating why B-tree indexes are the more broadly useful default."}}
{"title":"Query Optimization","summary":"A query optimizer's job is to take a declarative SQL query (specifying only what result is wanted) and choose an efficient execution plan (which indexes, which join algorithm, in what order) from among many logically-equivalent options that produce the same correct result but with vastly different performance. Join order matters enormously for multi-table queries: joining a small filtered table first, then joining against a larger table, can be orders of magnitude cheaper than the reverse, since intermediate result size compounds at each step — for n tables, the number of possible join orders grows factorially, which is why optimizers use heuristics and dynamic programming rather than exhaustively trying every order. There are several join algorithms an optimizer chooses between: nested loop join compares every row of one table against every row of the other (simple, O(n×m), efficient only when one side is small or indexed), hash join builds an in-memory hash table from the smaller table's join column and probes it while scanning the larger table (efficient for large unsorted equi-joins, roughly O(n+m)), and merge join takes advantage of both inputs already being sorted on the join column, merging them in one linear pass (efficient specifically when data is already sorted, such as from a B-tree index). Modern optimizers are cost-based: they maintain statistics about each table (row counts, value distributions, index selectivity) and estimate each candidate plan's cost before choosing the cheapest, which is why the same query can get a worse plan if statistics become stale relative to current data. Most database systems expose an EXPLAIN command showing the actual execution plan chosen, the standard diagnostic tool for understanding why a query is slow and whether an added index, rewritten query, or refreshed statistics would help.","learning_outcomes":["Explain why join order affects performance for a multi-table query, and why optimizers use heuristics or dynamic programming rather than exhaustive search.","Compare nested loop, hash, and merge join algorithms in terms of when each is most efficient, referencing table size, sortedness, and indexing.","Explain what role table statistics play in cost-based optimization, and describe a scenario where stale statistics lead to a poor plan."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming a written SQL query directly determines the exact execution steps — SQL is declarative, and the optimizer is free to choose any logically-equivalent plan, including a different join order than the query's literal text order.","Assuming nested loop join is always the worst choice — for small tables or a well-indexed small side, it can outperform the setup overhead of building a hash table or sorting for a merge join.","Believing a good execution plan stays good indefinitely — as data changes, statistics can become stale, causing the optimizer to make progressively worse estimates until statistics are refreshed.","Assuming merge join is always better than hash join since it needs no in-memory structure — its efficiency depends on inputs already being sorted; sorting unsorted inputs first can make it slower overall than a hash join."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Orders has 1,000,000 rows; Customers has 1,000 rows (10 in California). Each customer averages 1,000 orders. Compare filtering Customers first versus joining the full tables first.","steps":["Approach A: filter Customers to California first — 1,000 rows scanned, reduced to 10 matching rows.","Approach A continued: join these 10 customers against Orders, producing roughly 10×1,000=10,000 result rows, with an intermediate working set of just 10 rows before the join.","Approach B: join the full Customers (1,000) against the full Orders (1,000,000) first, producing a full intermediate result of roughly 1,000×1,000=1,000,000 rows.","Approach B continued: filter this 1,000,000-row result down to California customers' orders, arriving at the same final 10,000 rows."],"result":"Both approaches produce the same 10,000 final rows, but Approach A's intermediate working set (10 rows) is 100,000 times smaller than Approach B's (1,000,000 rows) — illustrating why join order matters, and why a cost-based optimizer using selectivity statistics would filter before joining."}}
{"title":"NoSQL & Distributed Databases","summary":"NoSQL databases emerged to address workloads where the relational model's rigid schema or strict consistency guarantees are a poor fit, trading some of SQL's guarantees for horizontal scalability (spreading data and load across many commodity machines) and schema flexibility (allowing records in the same collection to have different fields). NoSQL systems are broadly grouped into four categories: key-value stores (mapping a unique key directly to an opaque value, optimized for fast simple lookups); document stores (storing semi-structured, typically JSON-like documents, keeping related fields together to avoid joins for common access patterns); column-family stores (organizing data by column internally, efficient for aggregating over a column across many rows and for wide, sparse tables); and graph databases (storing data explicitly as nodes and relationships, optimized for traversal-heavy queries like 'friends of friends' that would need many expensive joins relationally). Horizontal scaling is typically achieved through sharding — partitioning data across multiple machines by some key (like a hash of the record's ID), so each machine holds only a portion of the total data and load. Many NoSQL systems relax strict consistency in exchange for availability and partition tolerance (a trade-off formalized by the CAP theorem, covered separately), commonly offering eventual consistency: after a write, replicas might temporarily disagree, but if no new writes occur, all replicas eventually converge — a deliberate, workload-appropriate trade-off rather than a flaw. Choosing between relational and NoSQL isn't about one being universally better, but about matching the data model, consistency requirements, and access patterns to the specific workload.","learning_outcomes":["Given a data access pattern (simple key lookups, nested related records, wide sparse tables, relationship traversal), identify which NoSQL category best fits it structurally.","Explain how sharding achieves horizontal scalability, and identify what determines which shard a given record is stored on.","Explain eventual consistency and describe a scenario where a read immediately after a write on a different replica could return stale data, and why this is acceptable."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming NoSQL databases are strictly 'better' or 'more modern' than relational — the choice depends on matching data model, consistency needs, and access patterns; relational remains the better fit for many transactional, interconnected data problems.","Assuming all NoSQL databases work the same way — the four categories have meaningfully different data models suited to different problems.","Assuming eventual consistency means data is unreliable — it's a deliberate, well-defined trade-off (bounded staleness resolving once writes stop) chosen for lower latency and higher availability.","Assuming sharding alone solves all scalability problems — a poorly chosen shard key can create hot spots, and queries spanning multiple shards become significantly more complex than a single-machine query."],"worked_example":{"setup":"A key-value store shards data across 4 machines using shard = hash(key) mod 4. Given hash('user:101')=53, hash('user:102')=78, hash('user:103')=61, hash('user:104')=100, determine each key's shard.","steps":["'user:101': 53 mod 4 = 1 (53 = 13×4+1) → shard 1.","'user:102': 78 mod 4 = 2 (78 = 19×4+2) → shard 2.","'user:103': 61 mod 4 = 1 (61 = 15×4+1) → shard 1.","'user:104': 100 mod 4 = 0 (100 = 25×4+0) → shard 0."],"result":"'user:101' and 'user:103' both land on shard 1 (a coincidental collision, not a problem — a shard commonly holds many keys); 'user:102' and 'user:104' land on shards 2 and 0 — each machine stores only its assigned portion of the total key space."}}
{"title":"Network Models (OSI / TCP-IP)","summary":"Network models organize the complex task of communicating between computers into distinct layers, each responsible for a specific concern and interacting only with the layers directly above and below it — this is what lets a web browser work identically over Wi-Fi, Ethernet, or a cellular connection, since the application layer doesn't need to know which physical medium carries the data. The OSI model defines seven layers: Physical (raw bit transmission), Data Link (framing and reliable transfer between directly connected nodes, using MAC addresses), Network (routing across networks, using IP addresses), Transport (end-to-end delivery between processes, using ports), Session (session setup/teardown), Presentation (format translation, encryption, compression), and Application (user-facing protocols like HTTP or DNS) — though it's primarily a teaching and reference model today rather than something protocols strictly match layer-for-layer. The TCP/IP model, what the actual internet is built on, condenses this into four layers: Link (Physical+Data Link combined), Internet (matching Network, home of IP), Transport (matching Transport, home of TCP and UDP), and Application (combining Session, Presentation, and Application). Data moving down through the layers undergoes encapsulation: each layer wraps data from the layer above with its own header (sometimes trailer), so an application message ends up wrapped in a Transport header, then Internet header, then Link header, before going out on the wire, and the receiving side strips these off in reverse order. This layered, modular design lets each layer evolve independently — a new physical medium doesn't require rewriting application protocols — and is the central organizing idea behind modern networking.","learning_outcomes":["List the seven OSI layers in order and state each layer's primary responsibility, and map each to its corresponding TCP/IP model layer.","Trace the encapsulation process for application data moving down through Transport, Internet/Network, and Link layers, identifying what header is added at each step.","Explain why layering allows independent evolution of different parts of the network stack, with a concrete example."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming every real protocol stack maps cleanly onto all seven OSI layers — the OSI model is primarily a teaching framework; the actual TCP/IP stack the internet uses has four layers.","Confusing MAC addresses (Data Link layer, identifying a network interface locally) with IP addresses (Network layer, identifying a host across interconnected networks).","Assuming encapsulation adds headers only — some layers (like Data Link, in Ethernet) also add a trailer after the payload, not just a header before it.","Believing a layer needs to understand the contents of layers above or below it — a layer only needs to correctly produce and consume its own header format, treating everything else as opaque data."],"worked_example":{"setup":"An application sends an HTTP GET request through the TCP/IP stack. Trace what headers are added moving down through Transport, Internet, and Link layers.","steps":["Application: the HTTP GET request (plain text) is the payload passed to Transport.","Transport: TCP wraps it with a header (source/destination port, sequence number), producing a TCP segment.","Internet: IP wraps the segment with a header (source/destination IP address), producing an IP packet.","Link: Ethernet wraps the packet with a header (source/destination MAC addresses) and a trailer (frame check sequence), producing an Ethernet frame."],"result":"The HTTP request ends up nested inside TCP, then IP, then Ethernet headers plus a trailer — the receiving computer strips these off in exactly the reverse order to recover the original request at its own Application layer."}}
{"title":"Physical & Data Link Layer","summary":"The physical layer transmits raw bits over a physical medium — copper wire, fiber, or radio — by encoding 1s and 0s as some physical signal, with no concept of 'frames' or 'addresses'; it simply carries a bit stream, with bandwidth, attenuation, and noise governing how fast and reliably it can do so. The data link layer sits above it and adds structure: it organizes the raw bit stream into frames, each carrying a source and destination MAC address (a hardware address burned into the network interface, uniquely identifying a device on a local network), and detects transmission errors using a checksum or Cyclic Redundancy Check (CRC) appended to each frame, computed from its contents and verified by the receiver. A switch operates at the data link layer, forwarding frames only to the port where the destination MAC address is known to be, rather than broadcasting to every device (what a simpler hub, at the physical layer, does) — letting a switched network scale to many devices without every device seeing every other's traffic. Multiple devices transmitting on a shared medium simultaneously causes a collision, corrupting both transmissions; a collision domain is the set of devices that could collide, and switches (unlike hubs) confine each collision domain to a single link, which is why modern switched Ethernet essentially never experiences collisions the way older shared-medium Ethernet did. Address Resolution Protocol (ARP) bridges the data link and network layers: given a destination IP address, ARP discovers the corresponding MAC address on the local network by broadcasting a request and receiving a reply, since a device ultimately needs the MAC address, not just the IP address, to address a data-link-layer frame.","learning_outcomes":["Explain the division of responsibility between the physical layer (raw bit transmission) and the data link layer (framing, addressing, error detection).","Explain why a switch can confine traffic to only the relevant port while a hub cannot, and describe the resulting scalability difference.","Explain what problem ARP solves and trace the broadcast-request/reply process by which a device discovers a MAC address."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming a MAC address and an IP address serve the same purpose — MAC identifies a device on its local network (data link layer), IP identifies a host across interconnected networks (network layer).","Confusing a switch with a hub — a hub blindly repeats every signal to every port (one shared collision domain), while a switch learns which MAC address is on which port and forwards only where needed.","Assuming error detection also means automatic error correction — a checksum lets a receiver detect a corrupted frame (to discard or request retransmission), not reconstruct the correct data from it.","Assuming ARP is needed for every destination — ARP resolves MAC addresses only for destinations on the same local network; a remote host instead resolves the local router's MAC address."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Device A (192.168.1.10) wants to send data to Device B (192.168.1.20) on the same local network but doesn't know B's MAC address. Trace the ARP process.","steps":["A checks its local ARP cache for 192.168.1.20 and finds no entry (a cache miss).","A broadcasts an ARP request to the whole local network: 'who has 192.168.1.20? tell 192.168.1.10'.","Every device receives the broadcast, but only B, recognizing its own IP, responds.","B sends a unicast ARP reply directly to A: '192.168.1.20 is at [B's MAC address]'."],"result":"Through one broadcast request and one unicast reply, A learns B's MAC address and can now send frames directly to it — and since A caches this mapping, it won't repeat the broadcast for subsequent packets until the cache entry expires."}}
{"title":"IP Addressing & Routing","summary":"An IPv4 address is a 32-bit number, written as four decimal numbers (0-255) separated by dots (dotted-decimal notation), split into a network portion (identifying which network a host belongs to) and a host portion (identifying the specific device) — the boundary is defined by a subnet mask or, more commonly today, CIDR notation, where a suffix like /24 means the first 24 bits are network, leaving 8 bits for hosts (up to 254 usable addresses, since the all-0s and all-1s host addresses are reserved for the network and broadcast addresses). Subnetting divides a larger network into smaller subnetworks by borrowing bits from the host portion to extend the network portion, letting an organization allocate address space matching each subnetwork's actual size (a /30 for a tiny 2-host link versus a /22 for a much larger department) rather than wastefully using the same fixed-size block regardless of need. A router forwards packets between networks by consulting its routing table, a list of destination network/prefix-length pairs paired with a next-hop address or interface — when a packet could match multiple entries with different prefix lengths, the router uses longest prefix match, always preferring the most specific matching entry. Because the 32-bit IPv4 address space (about 4.3 billion addresses) has been effectively exhausted relative to internet-connected devices, techniques like NAT (letting many devices share one public IP) have extended IPv4's practical lifespan, while IPv6, with a vastly larger 128-bit space, is the long-term replacement, though the transition has been gradual with both protocols coexisting. Understanding IP addressing and routing explains how a packet finds its way across the interconnected patchwork of independently-administered networks making up the internet, one router-to-router hop at a time.","learning_outcomes":["Given an IP address and CIDR prefix length (e.g. 192.168.1.0/26), determine the network address, broadcast address, and number of usable host addresses.","Given a routing table with multiple entries of different prefix lengths matching a destination, apply longest prefix match to determine which entry a router would use.","Explain why subnetting allows more efficient address space allocation than using a single fixed subnet size for every segment."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming a larger CIDR prefix number represents a larger network — a larger number means fewer host bits and a smaller subnet (/28 is smaller than /24), the reverse of what might seem intuitive.","Forgetting to subtract the reserved network and broadcast addresses when computing usable hosts — a subnet with h host bits has 2^h total addresses but only 2^h−2 usable ones.","Assuming a router picks the first matching table entry rather than the most specific one — longest prefix match always chooses the entry with the longest (most specific) prefix.","Assuming IPv4 exhaustion means the internet has literally run out of addresses — NAT lets many devices share one public address, extending IPv4's practical usability well past raw allocation exhaustion."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Given the network 192.168.1.0/26, determine the subnet mask, network address, broadcast address, and number of usable host addresses.","steps":["/26 means 26 network bits, leaving 32−26=6 host bits, so the subnet has 2⁶=64 total addresses.","Subnet mask: 26 ones then 6 zeros = 255.255.255.192 (4th octet's first 2 bits set: 128+64=192).","Network address: the given address already has all host bits at 0, so the network address is 192.168.1.0 itself.","Broadcast address: set all 6 host bits to 1, keeping network bits unchanged — 4th octet becomes 00111111 binary = 63, giving 192.168.1.63.","Usable hosts: 2⁶−2 = 62 (excluding network and broadcast), spanning 192.168.1.1 through .62."],"result":"The /26 subnet has mask 255.255.255.192, network address 192.168.1.0, broadcast address 192.168.1.63, and 62 usable host addresses — showing how prefix length alone determines every other property of the subnet."}}
{"title":"TCP & Congestion Control","summary":"TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) provides a reliable, ordered, connection-oriented byte-stream service on top of IP's unreliable, best-effort delivery, while UDP (User Datagram Protocol) provides a connectionless, unreliable datagram service with no ordering guarantees, trading reliability for lower overhead and latency — the choice depends on whether an application needs guaranteed in-order delivery (TCP, e.g. web browsing) or can tolerate loss for lower latency (UDP, e.g. live video streaming, where a late retransmitted packet is often less useful than simply skipping it). A TCP connection begins with a three-way handshake: the client sends a SYN, the server responds with a SYN-ACK (acknowledging the client's SYN while sending its own), and the client responds with an ACK, establishing initial sequence numbers before any data is sent. TCP achieves reliability using sequence numbers (every byte is numbered, letting the receiver detect missing or out-of-order data) and acknowledgments (unacknowledged data is retransmitted after a timeout), while flow control uses a receive window (advertised by the receiver, indicating how much unacknowledged data it's willing to buffer) to prevent a fast sender from overwhelming a slow receiver. Congestion control is a related but distinct concern — protecting the network itself, not just the receiver — implemented through a congestion window that starts small and grows during slow start (roughly doubling each round-trip until a threshold or loss), then grows more conservatively during congestion avoidance (linearly, by one segment per round-trip), and on detecting loss (interpreted as a congestion signal), the window is cut sharply, a pattern called Additive Increase, Multiplicative Decrease (AIMD) that lets TCP connections converge toward sharing available bandwidth fairly without explicit coordination.","learning_outcomes":["Explain the three-way handshake and what each of the three segments (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK) accomplishes in establishing a TCP connection.","Distinguish flow control from congestion control by explaining what each protects against and what signal each responds to.","Trace the congestion window's behavior across slow start, congestion avoidance, and a loss event, and explain why the pattern is called Additive Increase, Multiplicative Decrease."],"common_mistakes":["Confusing flow control with congestion control — flow control protects the receiver (based on its advertised buffer space), congestion control protects the network itself (based on inferred conditions like packet loss).","Assuming TCP's reliability means data is never actually lost in transit — TCP guarantees delivered data is complete and in order by detecting loss and retransmitting, not by making network loss impossible.","Assuming the congestion window grows the same way throughout a connection — it grows roughly exponentially during slow start and only linearly during congestion avoidance.","Assuming UDP is simply 'a worse version of TCP' — UDP deliberately omits reliability overhead because some applications benefit more from low latency than from guaranteed, possibly-delayed, in-order delivery."],"worked_example":{"setup":"A TCP connection's congestion window (cwnd) starts at 1 segment. Trace cwnd for 4 RTTs under slow start (doubling each RTT), then a loss at RTT 5 that halves the window.","steps":["RTT 1: cwnd = 1 segment.","RTT 2: cwnd doubles: 1×2 = 2 segments.","RTT 3: cwnd doubles: 2×2 = 4 segments.","RTT 4: cwnd doubles: 4×2 = 8 segments.","RTT 5: loss detected — AIMD halves cwnd: 8÷2 = 4 segments."],"result":"cwnd grows 1→2→4→8 across the first four RTTs (exponential growth during slow start), then is cut to 4 upon loss — after this, TCP typically switches to the slower, linear growth of congestion avoidance rather than resuming slow start's exponential growth."}}
{"title":"Application Layer Protocols (HTTP/DNS)","summary":"HTTP (HyperText Transfer Protocol) is a request-response protocol: a client sends a request specifying a method (GET to retrieve, POST to submit data, PUT to update/replace, DELETE to remove) and a target URL, and the server responds with a status code (2xx success, 3xx redirection, 4xx client errors like the famous 404 Not Found, 5xx server errors) along with content or an error description — HTTP is stateless, treating each request independently with no memory of previous requests, which is why cookies exist specifically to layer session tracking on top of an inherently stateless protocol. HTTPS is HTTP layered on top of TLS (Transport Layer Security), which encrypts the connection and verifies the server's identity via a certificate, without changing HTTP's own request-response semantics underneath. DNS (Domain Name System) translates human-readable domain names into IP addresses, organized as a hierarchical, distributed database: resolution typically starts at a root server (knowing where to find each top-level domain's servers), proceeds to a top-level-domain (TLD) server, and reaches the authoritative name server for that specific domain, which holds the actual records. DNS records serve different purposes: an A record maps a name directly to an IPv4 address, a CNAME record aliases one name to another for the lookup to continue from, and an MX record specifies the mail server for a domain — and because repeating this chain for every request would be slow, resolved records are cached for a duration specified by the record's TTL (Time To Live). Because both HTTP and DNS sit at the application layer, they rely on lower layers (TCP for HTTP's reliable delivery, typically UDP for DNS's fast, small queries) to actually move their data, focusing purely on defining the meaning and structure of the messages exchanged.","learning_outcomes":["Given an HTTP interaction, identify the method used, explain its purpose, and interpret a given status code's category and meaning.","Trace the DNS resolution process for a domain name from root server through TLD server to authoritative server.","Distinguish an A record, a CNAME record, and an MX record by the purpose each serves, and explain TTL's role in DNS caching."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming HTTP inherently remembers information between requests — HTTP is stateless; apparent 'memory' across requests (a shopping cart, a login session) is implemented separately, typically via cookies.","Confusing what HTTPS protects — TLS encrypts content in transit and verifies server identity, but doesn't change HTTP's own methods, status codes, or statelessness.","Assuming DNS resolution always requires contacting root, TLD, and authoritative servers fresh for every lookup — caching (via each record's TTL) answers most lookups from a cached copy without repeating the full chain.","Confusing a CNAME record with an A record — a CNAME points to another domain name (requiring a further lookup), while an A record maps directly to an IPv4 address."],"worked_example":{"setup":"A client looks up 'blog.example.com', configured as a CNAME to 'hosting.example.net', which has an A record pointing to 203.0.113.5. Trace the resolution.","steps":["The client queries a resolver for 'blog.example.com'.","The resolver finds a CNAME record: 'blog.example.com' aliases 'hosting.example.net' — the lookup continues with the new name.","The resolver looks up 'hosting.example.net' and finds an A record mapping it to 203.0.113.5.","The resolver returns both the CNAME relationship and the final resolved IP, 203.0.113.5."],"result":"Resolving 'blog.example.com' required one CNAME hop before reaching an A record — the client ultimately connects to 203.0.113.5, showing why a CNAME always requires at least one additional lookup step compared to a direct A record."}}
{"title":"Network Security Fundamentals","summary":"Network security aims to protect three core properties, the CIA triad: confidentiality (only authorized parties can read data), integrity (data hasn't been tampered with), and availability (systems remain accessible for legitimate use) — most security mechanisms and attacks can be understood by which of these three properties they protect or violate. Encryption is the primary tool for confidentiality, in two families: symmetric encryption uses the same secret key for encrypting and decrypting, fast but requiring securely sharing that key beforehand, while asymmetric (public-key) encryption uses a mathematically related key pair — a public key that can be shared openly to encrypt, and a private key, kept secret, that alone can decrypt — solving key distribution at the cost of being much slower, which is why systems like TLS use asymmetric encryption briefly at connection start (to establish a shared secret) and then switch to fast symmetric encryption for the actual data. Integrity is typically protected using cryptographic hash functions (producing a fixed-size fingerprint such that even a tiny input change produces a completely different hash, letting a receiver detect tampering by recomputing and comparing) and digital signatures (created with a sender's private key, verified with their public key, proving both integrity and authenticity, since only the key-holder could have produced a valid signature). A Man-in-the-Middle (MITM) attack occurs when an attacker secretly intercepts communication between two parties who believe they're communicating directly, which is precisely what TLS's certificate-based server authentication is designed to prevent, while a Denial-of-Service (DoS) attack targets availability directly, overwhelming a system until it can no longer serve legitimate users — a Distributed Denial-of-Service (DDoS) attack is the same idea from many sources simultaneously, making it far harder to block by filtering one source. Because no single mechanism protects all three CIA properties at once, real systems layer multiple defenses together, and security is best understood as a set of trade-offs and defense-in-depth practices rather than a single feature to simply 'turn on'.","learning_outcomes":["Given a described security incident or mechanism, classify which CIA triad property it primarily involves.","Explain the trade-off between symmetric and asymmetric encryption, and describe why TLS uses both together rather than just one.","Distinguish a Man-in-the-Middle attack from a Denial-of-Service attack in terms of which CIA property each threatens and what each attack actually does."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming encryption alone guarantees data integrity — encryption protects confidentiality, but doesn't by itself prove data hasn't been altered; integrity requires a separate mechanism like a hash or signature.","Assuming asymmetric encryption is used for all data in a TLS connection — it's computationally expensive, so real systems establish a shared secret with it and then switch to fast symmetric encryption for the bulk of the data.","Confusing a digital signature with simple encryption — a signature is created with the sender's private key and verified with their public key, the reverse key usage from typical encryption.","Assuming a DDoS attack is mechanically different from a DoS attack rather than a difference in scale — a DDoS is the same goal (overwhelming a target) carried out from many distributed sources, which is what makes it harder to block."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Classify each scenario by CIA property: (1) an attacker reads sensitive emails without authorization; (2) an attacker modifies a financial transaction's amount in transit; (3) an attacker floods a web server until legitimate users can't load the site.","steps":["Scenario 1: unauthorized reading of data — threatens confidentiality.","Scenario 2: unauthorized alteration of data — threatens integrity.","Scenario 3: legitimate users denied access to an available system — threatens availability."],"result":"Each scenario maps to a distinct CIA property — unauthorized reading (confidentiality), unauthorized modification (integrity), denial of access (availability) — and real incidents often combine more than one, which is why comprehensive security requires addressing all three rather than treating them as interchangeable."}}
